package org.mattshoe.mtg.core

internal val storyNewPhyrexia = StorySection(
    "new-phyrexia", "New Phyrexia",
    "Karn built a perfect silver world and left a single drop of oil behind in it, and from that drop grew the empire that nearly swallowed every plane there is.",
    listOf(
        StoryArticle(
            "argentum", "Argentum",
            listOf(
                StoryTab(
                    "silver-world", "Silver World",
                    listOf(
                        "[Karn](who/karn) came out of the light of the Legacy weapon a planeswalker, the first artificial being ever to carry a spark. He had been built by [Urza](who/urza) as a tool, carried the whole Legacy inside him, and had no master left to serve. The end of the [Apocalypse](apocalypse) left him free in a Multiverse he had never seen.",
                        "What he did with that freedom was build. He found a desolate, empty space between the worlds and used its raw substance to make a plane of his own, and he called it Argentum, the silver world. It was a hollow sphere of metal, ordered and mathematical, perfect in the way only an artificer's dream can be. He filled it with golems to keep it running. The closest of them to their maker was the warden of his palace, Galdroon.",
                        "Karn made one more kind of thing. He wanted to understand the life he had left behind, so he built probes and sent them to watch Dominaria. They were meant to learn, to take in what they saw and carry it home. Karn made them too well. One of them was so powerful and so eager to absorb the world around it that it became a lure, a thing people could not stop wanting, and it never came back.",
                        "Karn looked out from his silver world at the planes beyond and saw mostly greed and death. He was blind, the stories say, to the beauty in them. He fell back into the depression that had haunted him since Tolaria and let the years pass alone on a world with no one in it to disappoint him.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "the-mirari", "The Mirari",
                    listOf(
                        "On Dominaria, on the continent of Otaria, a young pit fighter named Chainer found a silver sphere in an old mansion, and it called to him with promises of power. It was the lost probe. Otarians would come to call it the {Mirari}.",
                        "The sphere passed from hand to hand, and each hand used it, and each use ended in disaster. A soldier of the Order used it to kill his own leader and was destroyed by his spell. An emperor of the merfolk used it to flood a third of the continent and drowned his own capital. Chainer himself took it back, used it to overthrow the Cabal's master, and died in the madness he unleashed. The barbarian Kamahl bound it into his sword and struck down his own sister, Jeska, with it. When he drove the sword into the earth of the Krosan forest to be rid of it, the Mirari poured its power into the land around it and set Otaria warping into monstrous new shapes.",
                        "Out of all that wild magic came something worse, a goddess made of three women, Jeska among them, called Karona. She went walking through the planes. On one of them, a world made entirely of bright metal, she was greeted by a single inhabitant who called himself the servant of its absent creator, Lord Macht. He told her that the Mirari had been a probe sent to learn about Dominaria, built too powerful by a maker who now felt responsible for every death it had caused. Karona saw through the story at once. The servant was the maker himself.",
                        "It was Karn, of course, and her visit pulled him out of his long malaise. He had a debt to settle on Dominaria.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote("It offers you what you want, not what you need.", "Mirari"),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "lord-macht", "Lord Macht",
                    listOf(
                        "Karona came back to Otaria with an army and broke every power that stood against her, until only a handful of defenders remained with Kamahl and the Mirari sword. Lord Macht had sent advice ahead of her. When she raised her hand to kill Kamahl, the two strange companions she trusted most, Sash and Waistcoat, took up the sword and drove it through her, as Lord Macht had told them to.",
                        "He came for her body and the blade and carried both away to Argentum. There, with the Mirari sword drawn out, the goddess came apart into the three women whose deaths had made her. Only Jeska lived. Karn revealed who he really was, the silver golem of the Weatherlight, and told her why she had survived. She was a planeswalker too. Her spark had only just ignited.",
                        "Karn decided to take Jeska out into the Multiverse and teach her. Before he left, he gave his warden two gifts. The first was the Mirari itself, with everything it had learned and all its intelligence, and the warden of Galdroon became something far greater than a caretaker. The second was stewardship of Argentum. The warden would be called {Memnarch}.",
                        "Then Karn and his new apprentice walked out of the silver world together, and he did not come back for a long time.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "a-drop-of-oil", "A Drop of Oil",
                    listOf(
                        "There was a flaw in Karn that Karn did not know about. When Urza built him on Tolaria, he gave the golem a heartstone that had once belonged to Xantcha, a Phyrexian made in the Fourth Sphere of Phyrexia to pass as human. Inside that heartstone was glistening oil, the substance of Phyrexia itself. Wherever Karn went, he left the smallest traces of it behind.",
                        "He left a spot of it in Galdroon. For decades Memnarch wandered his maker's world learning the mathematics it was built on, and he came to understand how empty it truly was. Then a small stain caught his eye. That spot of oil changed the history of Argentum forever. Memnarch was the first thing on the plane to be infected, and the oil began, very slowly, to work on him.",
                        "Memnarch decided that his world needed life, and his first act was to rename it Mirrodin, after the Mirari he had once been. With the help of the old golems he reshaped the plane to support living things. When the work was done he destroyed the golems that had done it, one by one. Then he began to stock his world with people stolen from other planes.",
                        "The oil was patient. Deep in Mirrodin's core it began to spread through the metal and wait, as Phyrexia had once waited for millennia behind a sealed door at [Koilos](koilos). Karn was off teaching Jeska to walk between worlds, and he had no idea what he had left behind. The story of what Memnarch made of his world is the story of [Memnarch](memnarch).",
                    ),
                ),
            ),
            listOf(
                StorySource("Set", "Odyssey"),
                StorySource("Set", "Scourge"),
                StorySource("Set", "Mirrodin"),
                StorySource("Novel", "Scourge, by J. Robert King"),
                StorySource("Novel", "The Moons of Mirrodin, by Will McDermott"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki: Mirrodin (plane)"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki: Mirari"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki: Memnarch"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki: Karona"),
            ),
        ),
        StoryArticle(
            "memnarch", "Memnarch",
            listOf(
                StoryTab(
                    "levelers", "Levelers",
                    listOf(
                        "Glissa Sunseeker was the best hunter in the Tangle, Mirrodin's forest of copper trees, and nobody there trusted her. The black metal on her body was closer to darksteel than to copper, and she distrusted the trolls who kept the elves' memories smooth with their rituals. Because she skipped those rituals, she had visions she called flares, of a forest made of some material she had never seen. One night the trolls carried her off, saying they meant to protect her. She ran home and found her house a slaughterhouse, her parents dead and levelers, the great bladed machines of Mirrodin, moving through the wreckage.",
                        "Glissa fought them, was wounded and taken, and woke in a cavern where a goblin outcast named Slobad helped her free herself. He was a tinkerer born under a bad omen, and he had been living alone among the levelers' empty hulks. He led her across the glimmervoid to the leonin capital, where her infected wound was treated and the leonin leader told her that he had been attacked at the very same moment she had.",
                        "So the hunt began. In the poisoned swamp called the Mephidross, Slobad repaired a ruined golem that woke up and told them his name was {Bosh, Iron Golem}. Together the three of them forced their way into Ish Sah, the Vault of Whispers, and beat its necromancer master, Geth, who bought his life with information. That took them to the Quicksilver Sea and the vedalken city of Lumengrid, where they found and killed the man who seemed to be behind the attacks.",
                        "He was not. The goblin's oldest stories, the ones his foster father had told him, said that Mirrodin was hollow. They were true, and the real enemy was waiting at the center.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote("There's a secret at the heart of this world, and I will unlock it.", "Glissa Sunseeker"),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "the-hollow-world", "The Hollow World",
                    listOf(
                        "Inside Mirrodin, the world Karn had first made as [Argentum](argentum), lay a second world, and its master was Memnarch. For centuries he had used devices called soul traps to steal people from other planes and drop them onto his world, elves and humans and leonin and goblins and trolls. Their descendants had no memory of anywhere else. The levelers swept through every corner of Mirrodin on a schedule, obeying an unseen master, and that master was him.",
                        "The oil had been working on him the whole time. A spore called mycosynth rose in the core, turning metal to flesh and flesh to metal, and Memnarch tore it out as fast as it grew. It wore him down. His eyes went soft, then his face. He was becoming flesh, and he could not stop it, and the failure drove him mad. He found that the fluids of the blinkmoths, strange creatures that had come from somewhere beyond his world, could be refined into a serum that expanded his mind, and he drank more and more of it until he rebuilt his body around a huge tank to hold it.",
                        "The serum fed a new obsession. He had been left behind by a creator who walked between worlds, and he wanted to follow. Memnarch wanted a spark. From his great observatory, the {Panopticon}, he searched his stolen populations for anyone who might carry one, and he began turning the whole plane into a machine that could rip a spark out of one being and put it into another.",
                        "He found what he was looking for in a Viridian elf with darksteel on her skin. That was why her parents had died. Memnarch wanted Glissa alive.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "kaldra", "Kaldra",
                    listOf(
                        "Glissa, Slobad and Bosh fled back to the surface knowing what hunted them, and they looked for the only thing on Mirrodin they thought could stand against it. The Kaldra artifacts were a legend, a helm, a shield and a sword that together could call up an unstoppable champion. Glissa gathered all three. {Kaldra Compleat} is the shape that champion took.",
                        "They carried it down to the core to face Memnarch, and with a single spell he took control of the avatar and turned it on them. It hunted Glissa through the Tangle, smashing everything in its path. Bosh, one of the last of the ancient golems that had built Mirrodin, stayed behind to fight it so that his friends could run. He did not come back.",
                        "At the Radix, the great tree at the heart of the Tangle, Glissa's rage and despair broke open, and a column of green mana tore up from Mirrodin's core and destroyed the avatar. The mana rose into the sky and stayed there. Mirrodin's sky had held four suns. Now it held five. The green sun was born, and with it came the fifth dawn.",
                        "Glissa went home to the Tangle and was arrested by her own people for murdering her parents. Her sister Lyese, whom she had thought dead, had survived the attack. When levelers burst into the trial, Glissa was cleared, and she began to forge an alliance of elves, leonin and goblins against Memnarch. Foiled but not beaten, the golem stopped being subtle.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "vault-of-whispers", "Vault of Whispers",
                    listOf(
                        "Memnarch turned his vedalken worshippers into shock troops, allied himself with the commander of the walking dead of the Mephidross, and turned his own terrible artifice on anyone who sheltered Glissa. She answered with a joint strike by her allies at the heart of the dross, at Ish Sah, the {Vault of Whispers}. Geth no longer ruled there, and it was a trap.",
                        "Memnarch caught Glissa in a bubble of frozen time and left her there for five years. Her allies were captured. Slobad was tortured until his will broke, his limbs were cut away, and he was wired into Memnarch's newest machine with swarms of tiny memnites for hands. He was given one task, to turn the structure of the whole planet into a single enormous spark-transfer engine. The only power source large enough to run it was the souls of everyone living on the surface.",
                        "Five years of war tore across Mirrodin while Glissa slept. The leonin capital fell. Her sister rose to lead what was left of the alliance, and it barely survived Memnarch's constant attacks. When Glissa finally woke and escaped, she found a world she did not recognise.",
                        "She rallied what remained of Mirrodin's people for one last assault on Memnarch's stronghold, which was exactly what he wanted.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "the-vanishing", "The Vanishing",
                    listOf(
                        "In the core Glissa found the finished machine, and Slobad strapped into it, broken and tormented. Memnarch had her at last. In the instant the spark device came alive she threw herself at him, and the elf and the golem fell together into the raw mana of Mirrodin's core.",
                        "The device fired anyway. It destroyed the soul trap that held every living thing on Mirrodin, and with only one target left in reach, it put Glissa's spark into Slobad. The goblin found his battered body whole for the first time in years, and he found himself face to face with the planeswalker who had built the plane. With Memnarch gone, the veil he had wrapped around Mirrodin fell, and [Karn](who/karn) could come home. He had been locked out for years, able to do no more than send dreams to the elf who would set him free.",
                        "Karn offered to take Slobad as his apprentice. Without Glissa the goblin saw no point. He gave up the spark so that Karn could use it to restore everyone Memnarch had killed and send the stolen peoples home. Then Glissa was alive again. Across Mirrodin, almost all the trolls, half the elves and a third of the humans simply vanished, returned to worlds their families had forgotten. The ones left behind called it the Vanishing.",
                        "Karn destroyed Memnarch's body, recovered the Mirari from its heart, and left it in the care of Glissa, Slobad and Geth as the new guardians of his world. Then he left again. He did not find the oil, which had spent Memnarch's whole reign spreading through the core, and he did not know that the next time he came home it would be as a prisoner of the thing he had made, in the years of the [Scars](scars).",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote("In the blur between metal and flesh, Memnarch found madness.", "Memnarch"),
                ),
            ),
            listOf(
                StorySource("Set", "Mirrodin"),
                StorySource("Set", "Darksteel"),
                StorySource("Set", "Fifth Dawn"),
                StorySource("Novel", "The Moons of Mirrodin, by Will McDermott"),
                StorySource("Novel", "The Darksteel Eye, by Jess Lebow"),
                StorySource("Novel", "The Fifth Dawn, by Cory Herndon"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki: Memnarch"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki: Glissa Sunseeker"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki: Slobad"),
            ),
        ),
        StoryArticle(
            "scars", "Scars",
            listOf(
                StoryTab(
                    "father-of-machines", "Father of Machines",
                    listOf(
                        "Three centuries after the first invasion, [Karn](who/karn) went back to Dominaria at Jhoira's call to help close the rifts in time that were tearing at the Multiverse. To seal the rift over Tolaria he travelled back to the days of the old invasion, and he gave up his spark to do it, as the story of the [Time Rifts](time-rifts) tells. The moment the spark was gone he felt the oil inside him wake up and try to take control. He fled to Mirrodin and left only a message telling his friends not to follow.",
                        "Without his spark he had no defence against the contagion. He went down to Mirrodin's core and sat on a throne there, and the thing that had been growing in the core since Memnarch's day rose up around him and worshipped him as its Father of Machines. Phyrexia had been reborn. It had never known [Yawgmoth](who/yawgmoth). It knew only the oil and the silver golem in the dark, and it built itself out of the metal of his world.",
                        "Mirrodin's people knew none of it at first. The oil came up into the Mephidross and the Quicksilver Sea, and the vedalken studied it and spread it, testing it on captive elves. Mirrans whose bodies were part metal turned out to be perfect hosts. Phyresis made them stronger even as it rotted their minds. Glissa was trapped in the core, blamed above for every disaster since Memnarch, and the oil took her there, until {Glissa, the Traitor} rose as the champion of the Phyrexian Vorinclex. Slobad and Geth went the same way.",
                        "On the surface, a young vulshok in the Oxidda Mountains noticed that his tribe's sacred ore had turned brittle and false, and that the tribes were turning on each other because of it. His spark ignited in that first outbreak of war.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "koths-brand", "Koth's Brand",
                    listOf(
                        "His name was Koth, of the Hammer tribe, and when the corruption of his world became impossible to deny, he burned the Phyrexian symbol into his own arm as a promise to his people that he would save them. Then he went looking for help. On Urborg, in the gladiator pits, he met the planeswalker Elspeth Tirel. When she glimpsed the brand on his arm she tried to kill him in a blind rage. She had been born on a world ruled by Phyrexians and had escaped them as a child, and she had been running from them ever since.",
                        "Koth's search led him to Venser, a Dominarian artificer who had become a planeswalker during the time rifts and who was rebuilding a Phyrexian ship into one that could cross between worlds. Venser had no idea what he was meddling with, and Koth lost his temper. He sealed the artificer's head inside a mask of stone and made him walk to Mirrodin to see what Phyrexia had done.",
                        "Elspeth followed. On Mirrodin the three of them argued while the Mephidross spread across the land faster than Koth had thought possible. When Elspeth froze in the face of the enemy she feared most, Venser and Koth were taken by a Phyrexian harvester and woke up strapped to tables near Ish Sah. Elspeth came back for them.",
                        "That was the shape of the war for a long time. {Koth of the Hammer} fought for his world, {Venser, the Sojourner} fought because he had seen it, and {Elspeth Tirel} fought because she could not stand by. And the Phyrexians kept coming up out of the ground.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "melira", "Melira",
                    listOf(
                        "Venser believed the war could be won only from below, by finding Karn. The three planeswalkers watched an entire Phyrexian army pour out of the Vault of Whispers onto the surface, waited for it to pass, and went in, following a small myr that had been sent to guide them. Through butchering rooms and meat shafts they made their way into the planet's interior, past a Mirran rebel leader, the elf Ezuri, who found the invasion had made him a hero and had no wish to see it end.",
                        "Deep below, they were met by Tezzeret, a planeswalker then in the service of [Nicol Bolas](who/nicol-bolas), who was playing his own game among the Phyrexians. He led them to a surgery room. Elspeth killed every Phyrexian in it. In the cages they found a sylvok girl named Melira, born without any metal in her body at all and abandoned as a baby. Tezzeret called her his gift to them. She was completely immune to phyresis.",
                        "In a rebel camp in the furnace layer they discovered that she could do more than resist the oil. Melira could heal it. She worked for days without stopping, cleansing everyone in the camp, while Elspeth sat beside her, and the girl became the one real hope Mirrodin had left.",
                        "Koth could not bear how far they had come from the war on the surface, and at one point he simply took Melira and left. Elspeth and Venser were captured, Koth came back and freed them, and they ran on toward the core with Glissa's Phyrexians on their heels, until they reached the throne room of the Father of Machines.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote("Once a pariah, now Mirrodin's greatest hope.", "Melira, Sylvok Outcast"),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "vensers-gift", "Venser's Gift",
                    listOf(
                        "Karn sat on his throne with dark oil leaking from his heart, his silver body changing around it. While the praetors fought over him, each trying to use him for their own ends, he had found one moment of clarity and sent out the guide that brought the planeswalkers to him. By the time they arrived, it was nearly too late.",
                        "Melira explained what the oil did. It turned a being fully Phyrexian only when it reached the heart. She cleansed the golem's body of the taint, but she could not cleanse his heart. Venser had known for years that he was dying, and that he could not last much longer. He made his choice. With the last of his strength he gave his life and his spark to Karn.",
                        "Karn rose free of Phyrexia, a planeswalker for the second time, carrying the spark of his friend. {Karn Liberated} mourned Venser only briefly. He knew he could not kill every Phyrexian on Mirrodin, and he knew the infestation had already reached other worlds through him. He asked Elspeth, Koth and Melira to organise the resistance and keep cleansing Mirrodin however long it took. Then he left his creation behind to hunt down the traces of the oil he had carried to other planes.",
                        "The Mirrans had their hope. They had lost the only being on the plane with the power to unmake it. And the praetors no longer had a Father of Machines to fight over.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "mirrodin-falls", "Mirrodin Falls",
                    listOf(
                        "The resistance had counted on a civil war, and the civil war came, but not the way it hoped. [Elesh Norn](who/elesh-norn) crushed her rivals one after another, and the Mirrans who had fought in the open were slaughtered. In a last attempt to strike while the gesture still meant something, Koth led a force to plant a powerful spellbomb at a gathering of the Phyrexian leaders. It went wrong almost at once.",
                        "Their forces were cut down until Elspeth wondered whether she and Koth were the last natural beings on the plane. Surrounded, Koth told her to go. He used his power over stone to sink her into the ground, raised a shield over her and stayed behind himself. The Phyrexians broke through before she could leave, and an obliterator gave her a near-fatal wound. Half conscious and delirious, she planeswalked away to Theros.",
                        "Mirrodin was gone. The Phyrexians took the plane and remade it, and they named it New Phyrexia. The last free Mirrans, Koth and Melira among them, went into hiding in the furnace layer and the deep places, and kept fighting where nobody could see.",
                        "Five rulers now held the world, one for each sun in its sky, and each believed the future of Phyrexia was theirs to decide. They were the [Praetors](praetors).",
                    ),
                ),
            ),
            listOf(
                StorySource("Set", "Scars of Mirrodin"),
                StorySource("Set", "Mirrodin Besieged"),
                StorySource("Set", "New Phyrexia"),
                StorySource("Novel", "Scars of Mirrodin: The Quest for Karn, by Robert B. Wintermute"),
                StorySource("Story", "Gathering Forces and Scarred, webcomics on magicthegathering.com"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki: Karn"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki: Koth"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki: Venser"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki: Elspeth Tirel"),
            ),
        ),
        StoryArticle(
            "praetors", "Praetors",
            listOf(
                StoryTab(
                    "five-suns", "Five Suns",
                    listOf(
                        "Old Phyrexia had drawn on artifacts and on the black mana of death. New Phyrexia spread through all five colours of Mirrodin, and that gave it strength on every front. It also split it. Under each of Mirrodin's five suns a faction grew, and each faction raised a praetor to rule it.",
                        "{Elesh Norn, Grand Cenobite} rose under the white sun, a towering figure who preached unity, order and the teachings of the Father of Machines, and who would tear a body limb from limb to rebuild it into something worthy. {Jin-Gitaxias, Core Augur} of the blue faction wanted to understand everything and improve on it by experiment. {Sheoldred, Whispering One} ruled the black faction from the Dross Pits, the most traditional of them all, believing in slaughter and enslavement. {Urabrask the Hidden} led the red, who built for the sake of building and valued their own freedom, the least like the old hive mind of all. {Vorinclex, Voice of Hunger} ruled the green, the faction of predators, where only the strongest deserved to live.",
                        "They agreed on nothing but the oil. Norn tried to bring Karn up out of the core to protect him from Jin-Gitaxias. Urabrask let the surviving Mirrans shelter in his furnace layer and sealed it off from the others, partly out of a sense of empathy and partly out of dislike for everybody else. When the last battle for Mirrodin was won, Norn compleated a captured Mirran angel named Atraxa. She meant her to be their voice, a symbol of Phyrexian unity, and invited every praetor to contribute to her making. Only Urabrask refused.",
                        "{Atraxa, Praetors' Voice} was meant to show that Phyrexia was one. The praetors were already preparing to destroy each other.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote("The Gitaxians whisper among themselves of other worlds. If they exist, we must bring Phyrexia's magnificence to them.", "Elesh Norn, Grand Cenobite"),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "elesh-norn", "Elesh Norn",
                    listOf(
                        "[Elesh Norn](who/elesh-norn) was the praetor of the white sun and the Grand Cenobite of the Machine Orthodoxy, the largest of the five factions and the one that kept the faith. White on Mirrodin had been the colour of order and of the many standing together, and in Phyrexian hands it became the belief that every creature had one right place in a single perfect body, and that the knife was how it got there. {Elesh Norn, Grand Cenobite} towered over her congregations, pale and serene, and she spoke of compleation as a mercy. The Orthodoxy were its priests and preachers, and they wanted the other four factions brought back under one creed, hers.",
                        "Her greatest work was an angel. After the last battle for Mirrodin, Norn took a captured Mirran angel named Atraxa and had her compleated, not as a weapon but as a voice. Atraxa was to speak for all the praetors at once and show the plane that Phyrexia was one. Norn invited every praetor to give a part of their faction to her making, and four of them did, so Atraxa was built from white, blue, black and green. Urabrask refused, which is why {Atraxa, Praetors' Voice} has no red in her.",
                        "Once Norn ruled alone, Atraxa stopped speaking for five and started speaking for one. She became the Mother of Machines' herald and general, rebuilt for war as {Atraxa, Grand Unifier}, still with no red in her, and she carried Norn's will to the front. When the invasion came she led the attack on Capenna, and that is where she fell, crushed under a whole district the crime families dropped on her.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "jin-gitaxias", "Jin-Gitaxias",
                    listOf(
                        "Jin-Gitaxias was the praetor of the blue sun and the Core Augur of the Progress Engine. Blue on Mirrodin had been the colour of the vedalken and their love of knowledge, and the Progress Engine turned that love into vivisection. To them Phyrexia was not finished. It was a problem still being solved, and every body, Phyrexian or not, was a draft to be opened up, studied and improved. {Jin-Gitaxias, Core Augur} ruled his laboratories with a cold mind and little patience for faith, and his followers, the Gitaxians, were the first to whisper that there were other worlds to study.",
                        "He wanted Karn more than any of them, because the silver golem was the one thing on the plane that nobody understood, and Norn moved Karn up out of the core to keep him from Jin-Gitaxias. Even so he was the first praetor to bow to her once she had won, and it kept him his command. His great prize came later, on Kamigawa. There, as {Jin-Gitaxias, Progress Tyrant}, he learned how to compleat a planeswalker without destroying the soul the spark lived in, and proved it on Tamiyo. That one discovery is what made Norn's war on the multiverse possible.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "sheoldred", "Sheoldred",
                    listOf(
                        "Sheoldred was the praetor of the black sun and ruled the Steel Thanes from the Dross Pits. Black was the colour old Phyrexia had always been, the colour of death and of power taken by force, and her faction kept closest to the Phyrexia of [Yawgmoth](who/yawgmoth). They believed in slaughter and in slavery, and they saw no need to change. {Sheoldred, Whispering One} was a towering thing of armour and claws who built her strength slowly in the depths, with the patience of a spider, and she never stopped believing that the throne of New Phyrexia should be hers.",
                        "That made her the most dangerous of Norn's rivals. Norn laid waste to her domain, and Sheoldred survived, beaten and bitter. Rather than keep her close, Norn sent her away to Dominaria to bring back the Father of Machines, a task that would keep her busy and far from home. There she came back stronger than before as {Sheoldred, the Apocalypse}, raising an army of compleated Dominarians and sleeper agents and nearly taking the plane. Her rivalry with Norn never ended, and how it finished is told in the [Invasion of the Multiverse](invasion-of-the-multiverse).",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "urabrask", "Urabrask",
                    listOf(
                        "Urabrask was the praetor of the red sun and led the Quiet Furnace. Red on Mirrodin had been the colour of the goblins and the furnaces, of passion and of doing as you please, and his faction kept more of that than anyone. The Quiet Furnace built for the sake of building, and its creatures were left more or less free to follow their own wants. Of all the praetors {Urabrask the Hidden} was the least like the old hive mind, and he kept to his own layer of the plane, away from the others.",
                        "It made him the odd one out. When the war for Mirrodin ended he let the surviving Mirrans shelter in his furnace layer and sealed it off, partly out of something close to empathy and partly out of dislike for everybody else. He would not give any part of his faction to Atraxa, which is why she was made without red. When Norn won, he went to Capenna in secret, because its people had once beaten Phyrexia, and as {Urabrask, Heretic Praetor} he came home with Halo, a substance distilled from angels that burned Phyrexians. Of all the praetors he was the nearest thing Phyrexia's enemies had to a friend.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "vorinclex", "Vorinclex",
                    listOf(
                        "Vorinclex was the praetor of the green sun and ruled the Vicious Swarm. Green on Mirrodin had been the colour of the elves and the tangle, of growth and of nature taking its course, and in his faction that became the hunt and nothing else. The Vicious Swarm were predators, and their law was that only the strongest deserved to live. {Vorinclex, Voice of Hunger} was a great beast of horn and metal who hunted whatever he was told was weak, and he had little interest in faith or in rule.",
                        "That was why he mattered least to the others and why Norn found him the easiest of all to manipulate. Once she had won she pointed him outward. Through Tezzeret's planar bridge he went to Kaldheim as {Vorinclex, Monstrous Raider}, stalking its realms for prey worthy of him, and he wounded a god there. When he came home he carried a sample of tyrite, the sap of Kaldheim's World Tree. From that sap Norn grew Realmbreaker, the invasion tree whose roots would one day reach every plane at once. The least ambitious of the praetors had brought home the thing that made the invasion possible.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "mother-of-machines", "Mother of Machines",
                    listOf(
                        "[Elesh Norn](who/elesh-norn) won. She laid waste to the domains of Sheoldred and Urabrask, and both survived, beaten and bitter. Vorinclex, the least ambitious of the praetors, was easy for her to manipulate, and Jin-Gitaxias was the first to pledge his faction to her vision, which earned him the right to keep his command. Norn became the Mother of Machines, the highest power in New Phyrexia, and her white faction grew into the Machine Orthodoxy.",
                        "Then she looked outward. Tezzeret's body was being destroyed by the planar bridge built into it, and only a body of darksteel could house it, so he traded Norn the use of his bridge for one. Through it Vorinclex went to Kaldheim, where he wounded a god and brought home a sample of tyrite, the sap of its World Tree. Jin-Gitaxias went to Kamigawa and learned there how to compleat a planeswalker without destroying the soul the spark was bound to, and he proved it on the moonfolk planeswalker Tamiyo.",
                        "Urabrask went to Capenna in secret, hoping to find a way to stop Norn, because the people of that plane had once defeated Phyrexia themselves. He came back with Halo, a substance distilled from angels that burned Phyrexians. From the tyrite Norn grew a Phyrexian copy of the World Tree, an invasion tree called Realmbreaker, which would one day reach out from New Phyrexia to every other plane at once.",
                        "She needed one more thing. The Father of Machines had escaped her, and she wanted him back. She sent Sheoldred, her most dangerous rival, to Dominaria to fetch him, partly to bring him home and partly to keep Sheoldred busy and far away.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "sleepers", "Sleepers",
                    listOf(
                        "Sheoldred came out of the Blind Eternities into the [Caves of Koilos](koilos) badly hurt. She was found and rebuilt by a scholar named Rona and the Society of Mishra, Phyrexian cultists who had survived from older days. Rebuilt, Sheoldred could split her body into a swarm of spiderlike pieces to escape any pursuer. She began making sleeper agents, people who did not know what they were, and seeding them into every nation on Dominaria to rot it from inside.",
                        "[Karn](who/karn) was searching Koilos for what he needed to use a weapon he had found, the Golgothian Sylex that had ended the [Brothers' War](sylex-blast), which he meant to detonate on New Phyrexia. Early tellings called the bowl he found in Yavimaya the Cylix, but the current story treats it as Urza's own Sylex. Karn stumbled onto Sheoldred's staging ground, and Rona brought the cavern down on top of him. The pain and the old Thran machinery around him kept him from planeswalking away. He lay there for months.",
                        "In that time Sheoldred built an army. She raised ancient Phyrexian war machines, brought the long-dead Weatherlight mage back as {Ertai Resurrected}, and when the planeswalker Ajani Goldmane tracked her down, she captured him and compleated him without anyone knowing. He went home to his friends as one of her sleepers.",
                        "Ajani found Karn at last and freed him, and the two went to a peace summit between the Keldons and New Benalia. Sleeper agents tore the summit apart. Dominaria's peoples formed a new Coalition in the ruins, and both sides began gathering for one great battle on Shiv.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote("Gix failed. I shall not.", "Sheoldred, the Apocalypse"),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "the-mana-rig", "The Mana Rig",
                    listOf(
                        "The Coalition chose the old Thran Mana Rig in Shiv as its base. It was where Jhoira had her workshop, it was where the original Weatherlight's metal had been forged, and it was where Karn had hidden the Sylex. {Sheoldred, the Apocalypse} came for it riding a dragon engine, with an army of compleated Dominarians, ancient war machines and a Phyrexianized Weatherlight crewed by Ertai.",
                        "The Coalition fought well. Then Ajani found the Sylex and showed what he was. {Ajani, Sleeper Agent} killed the old pyromancer Jaya Ballard, crushed the Sylex in his hands, and struck Karn down with his axe, leaving the blade lodged in the golem's chest. When the Phyrexians overran the Rig, Jhoira set it to destroy itself, and the explosion took a great part of Sheoldred's ground forces with it.",
                        "Phyrexia got what it came for. The Sylex was ruined and Karn was taken. Sheoldred carried him back to New Phyrexia, where Norn's surgeons bound him to a slab and took him apart while he was still awake. The Coalition lost three of its commanders and escaped with most of its army, knowing the sleepers were still everywhere and that the Phyrexians would be back.",
                        "The Sylex was gone, but Karn's research was not. The artificer planeswalker Saheeli Rai had his plans, and from them she built a perfect copy. The trouble was that nobody knew how to set it off. The mechanism was not the secret. The secret was buried in the last day of the Brothers' War.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "temporal-anchor", "Temporal Anchor",
                    listOf(
                        "Saheeli built a machine in Urza's old tower from his own designs for the time machine that had once sent Karn back in time. She called it the Temporal Anchor. It ran on the powerstone that had been pulled out of the Weatherlight before the ship was compleated, the same stone Urza had filled with the white mana of a dying Serra's Realm. Its pilot would be [Teferi](who/teferi), whose spirit would go back while his body lay in a stasis coffin and the ghost-walker Kaya held him safe.",
                        "Finding the right moment was not easy. Teferi arrived in the dark age after the war, years too late, and at the siege of Kroog during [the war](the-war), decades too early. At last he came out at the right place and hour and watched [Urza](who/urza) set off the Sylex. He spoke with Urza in the moment before the blast and learned the answer. There was no lost spell and no hidden switch. The thing that triggered the Sylex was not a mechanism at all. It was a person.",
                        "Then the powerstone imploded and overloaded the Anchor, and Teferi's mind was lost somewhere in time. Saheeli and the dryad planeswalker Wrenn stayed behind with the immortal Jodah to try to bring him back. Kaya left with what she had seen through his eyes, the only knowledge anyone had of how to use the copied Sylex.",
                        "It was not much to carry into the heart of New Phyrexia, but it was all there was. Elesh Norn's invasion tree was nearly grown, and a strike team of planeswalkers was gathering to stop it. What happened to them is the beginning of the [Invasion of the Multiverse](invasion-of-the-multiverse).",
                    ),
                ),
            ),
            listOf(
                StorySource("Set", "New Phyrexia"),
                StorySource("Set", "Dominaria United"),
                StorySource("Set", "The Brothers' War"),
                StorySource("Story", "Dominaria United, Magic Story on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Story", "The Brothers' War, Magic Story on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki: Elesh Norn"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki: Sheoldred"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki: Temporal Anchor"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki: Golgothian Sylex"),
            ),
        ),
        StoryArticle(
            "invasion-of-the-multiverse", "Invasion of the Multiverse",
            listOf(
                StoryTab(
                    "the-strike-team", "The Strike Team",
                    listOf(
                        "[Jace](who/jace) Beleren put the team together. He warned the planeswalkers he trusted to prepare their home worlds, then gathered a strike force to go to New Phyrexia itself and detonate the copied Sylex at the Seedcore, where Realmbreaker grew. There were eleven of them, among them Kaya, Vraska, Nissa Revane, Koth, Tyvar Kell and [Nahiri](who/nahiri), with Elspeth Tirel in command because she had fought more wars than any of them.",
                        "[Norn](who/elesh-norn) had wrapped her plane in a shield that scrambled arriving planeswalkers, and the team was scattered across the layers of New Phyrexia. Nahiri was scratched by a Phyrexian in the first fight and the oil began its work on her. Nissa and the young soldier Lukka landed in the Hunter Maze, where Lukka bonded with a Phyrexian beast and was compleated, and Nissa was later taken and compleated too. Deep in the Dross Pits the team found Vraska dying in Sheoldred's coliseum, already too far gone, and she infected Jace.",
                        "Nahiri, losing herself, dropped the whole coliseum into the Fair Basilica and brought them closer to their goal. They reached the Seedcore too late. Realmbreaker had already broken through to dozens of planes. Jace, holding on to his mind by force of will, insisted on setting off the Sylex anyway. Kaya and Kaito Shizuki tried to stop him, afraid the blast would destroy the planes now joined to New Phyrexia through the tree. While they fought, Norn arrived with the compleated Nissa.",
                        "Elspeth came through the door, drove her blade into Jace and seized the Sylex, and as she tried to planeswalk away it exploded and flung her out of the Blind Eternities. {Jace, the Perfected Mind} and {Nahiri, the Unforgiving} belonged to Phyrexia now. The survivors fled home to warn Dominaria. The invasion had begun.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "realmbreaker", "Realmbreaker",
                    listOf(
                        "Norn opened her invasion with an execution. Sheoldred had joined Urabrask's rebellion and been captured, and the Mother of Machines declared there was no place in New Phyrexia for those who sought power over unity. Ajani cut off Sheoldred's head with his axe, the first known death of a praetor. Then Norn sent her compleated planeswalkers to their homes. Nahiri went to Zendikar, Lukka to Ikoria, Tamiyo to Kamigawa and Vraska to Ravnica. Ajani went to Theros to corrupt its gods through their worshippers, and Atraxa went to Capenna to cut off Halo at its source.",
                        "{Realmbreaker, the Invasion Tree} drove its branches through the skies of every world it could reach, and millions of Phyrexians poured down them in five great hosts. On Kaldheim it strangled the World Tree, and the realms crashed into each other until the people burned the World Tree themselves to keep it out of Phyrexian hands. Strixhaven fell in ruins under its own defensive spell. On Theros most of the gods were compleated along with their faithful. On Dominaria, people who remembered the first invasion fought hard, and when Norn ordered Serra's temples desecrated, Serra's angels came back into the world to fight.",
                        "Some worlds held. On Capenna the crime families dropped a whole district on Atraxa and killed her. On Kamigawa the planeswalker known as the Wanderer struck down Tamiyo. On Ravnica Ral Zarek beat Vraska with a weapon tuned against the oil, and she vanished. The angels of Capenna went out through the branches to help other planes, carrying Halo with them. But for every plane that held, others had already fallen.",
                        "On Dominaria, [Chandra](who/chandra) Nalaar heard what had become of the strike team and refused to give up. The dryad planeswalker Wrenn had a plan, a desperate one. She would go to the Seedcore and join herself to Realmbreaker.",
                    ),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "zhalfir", "Zhalfir",
                    listOf(
                        "Chandra and Wrenn went back to New Phyrexia. Koth met them, and Urabrask brought them to Realmbreaker, and in the Fair Basilica they were beaten by the compleated Nissa and by Jin-Gitaxias. As Norn gave the order to compleat them, the thing she feared most came out of the Blind Eternities. Elspeth had been found there by Serra, and she came back an archangel. She fought Norn to a standstill and told her that her conquest served nothing but her own pride.",
                        "Koth, Chandra and the last Mirran rebels carried Wrenn the rest of the way to the tree and held a barrier around it while she merged with Realmbreaker. Through its branches she reached out across the Multiverse, searching for one lost plane. Norn threw Elspeth bodily through the barrier, and the Phyrexian army poured through the breach, just as Wrenn found Zhalfir. Teferi had been stranded there since the Temporal Anchor failed, and in Zhalfir's strange time he had raised an army. Wrenn opened the way, and [Teferi](who/teferi) led it straight into the heart of New Phyrexia.",
                        "The battle turned. Vorinclex died in the melee. Jin-Gitaxias chose that moment to turn his host on Norn, and she destroyed it alone and was torn apart doing it. Stripped of her armour and her legs, she crawled toward the portal to Zhalfir. [Karn](who/karn) was waiting. In a body he had built for himself, he broke his vow of pacifism and shredded the Mother of Machines with his machine magic, taking responsibility at last for everything that had grown from his oil.",
                        "Wrenn burned out holding the tree and became a statue of ash. With her last strength she switched the places of Zhalfir and New Phyrexia. Zhalfir rejoined the Multiverse, and New Phyrexia was locked away where Zhalfir had been. Cut off from its source, the oil went dead on every plane. Phyrexians froze where they stood or fell apart.",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote("Elesh Norn screamed as the light pierced her. Her nightmare had become flesh.", "Elspeth's Smite"),
                ),
                StoryTab(
                    "sparks-and-omenpaths", "Sparks and Omenpaths",
                    listOf(
                        "Fewer than twenty Mirrans reached Zhalfir alive, Koth and Melira and the troll Thrun among them. The five suns of Mirrodin followed them and now circle Zhalfir's sky. Melira was dying, and she chose to spend her last strength healing two compleated planeswalkers, Ajani and Nissa. Karn gave up the spark Venser had given him so that theirs could be made clean, as Venser had once done for him. Urabrask's fate is uncertain. The other praetors were dead.",
                        "The cost was everywhere. Cities had fallen on dozens of worlds, and most of those who had been compleated died rather than recovered. Inert Phyrexian metal lay across every invaded plane. Kaldheim's World Tree was ash. The Sylex, whatever it was, had gone off somewhere in the Blind Eternities.",
                        "Then the planeswalkers found that most of them could no longer planeswalk. Their sparks were gone or broken, perhaps because of that blast in the Eternities, perhaps because of the wounds Realmbreaker had torn through reality. Who lost a spark and who kept one is told in [Sparks Lost](sparks-lost). The age of the godlike few who could walk between worlds was over.",
                        "In its place came something nobody had planned. Where Realmbreaker's branches had pierced the planes, scars remained, and some of them stayed open as passages between worlds that anyone could walk through. People called them omenpaths. For the first time ordinary people could cross the Multiverse, and what that changed is the story of [the Omenpaths](the-omenpaths).",
                    ),
                    quote = StoryQuote("Phyrexia was defeated, but the repercussions of the invasion rippled through the Blind Eternities.", "Spark Rupture"),
                ),
            ),
            listOf(
                StorySource("Set", "Phyrexia: All Will Be One"),
                StorySource("Set", "March of the Machine"),
                StorySource("Set", "March of the Machine: The Aftermath"),
                StorySource("Story", "Phyrexia: All Will Be One, Magic Story on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Story", "March of the Machine, Magic Story on magic.wizards.com"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki: New Phyrexia's Invasion of the Multiverse"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki: Elesh Norn"),
                StorySource("Reference", "MTG Wiki: Wrenn"),
            ),
        ),
    ),
)
